<Wiki
  title="puv check"
  description="Check PHP syntax and whether the project files agree with each other."
>
  <p class="kicker">Wiki</p>
  <h1>check</h1>
  <p>
    <code>puv check</code> checks syntax and project consistency. It prints a summary even when
    nothing is wrong, so a clean run is still visible.
  </p>

  <h2>A healthy project</h2>
  <pre class="block">puv init
puv use 8.4
puv add symfony/console
puv check</pre>
  <p>
    The summary covers the project you just built: the manifest, the lock, and the PHP sources.
    A quiet command would be easy to mistake for a skipped check. <code>check</code> does not
    stay quiet.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you package</h2>
  <pre class="block">puv check
puv build</pre>
  <p>
    Run it before <a href="/wiki/commands/build"><code>puv build</code></a> or
    <a href="/wiki/commands/package"><code>puv package</code></a> when you want that summary
    first. <code>check</code> does not write <code>dist/</code>. A manifest that no longer
    matches the lock is what stops <a href="/wiki/commands/sync"><code>puv sync</code></a>, which
    then tells you to run <a href="/wiki/commands/lock"><code>puv lock</code></a>.
  </p>
</Wiki>
